BEGIN;

INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: easy / L2 — Hashing vs encryption for password storage
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In January 2026, Falcon Security Corp. needs to store user passwords in its authentication database. The security architect selects a method that produces a fixed-length output from which the original value cannot be recovered. Which technique is the architect implementing?', '["Hashing", "Symmetric encryption", "Tokenization", "Steganography"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Per NIST SP 800-63B, passwords should be stored using one-way hash functions that produce irreversible fixed-length digests. Hashing is correct because it is a one-way function — the original input cannot be derived from the output, making it ideal for password storage. Unlike symmetric encryption (choice B), which is reversible with the correct key, hashing provides no mechanism for recovering the plaintext value.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Hardware Security Module (HSM) for key management
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In March 2026, Pinnacle Financial Services processes 2.3 million credit card transactions monthly and must comply with PCI DSS requirements for cryptographic key storage. The CISO evaluates a tamper-resistant physical device dedicated to generating, storing, and managing encryption keys. Which technology is the CISO evaluating?', '["Trusted Platform Module (TPM)", "Hardware Security Module (HSM)", "Software-based Key Management Service (KMS)", "Virtual private network (VPN)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Per NIST SP 800-57 Part 1 and PCI DSS Requirement 3.5, Hardware Security Modules (HSMs) are tamper-resistant hardware devices specifically designed for cryptographic key generation, storage, and management. HSMs are correct because they provide FIPS 140-2/3 validated physical protection for keys in high-volume payment environments. Unlike a TPM (choice A), which is embedded in endpoints for device-level attestation rather than enterprise-scale key management, HSMs are purpose-built for centralized cryptographic operations.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Hold Your Own Key (HYOK) evaluation
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In February 2026, NovaTech Industries stores regulated financial data across three cloud providers. The Chief Security Officer must recommend a key management strategy that ensures the company retains exclusive control over encryption keys — including the ability to revoke cloud provider access immediately — without relying on any cloud-hosted key infrastructure. Which strategy should the CSO recommend?', '["Cloud-provider-managed encryption keys with automatic rotation", "Bring Your Own Key (BYOK) uploaded to the cloud provider''s KMS", "Hold Your Own Key (HYOK) with on-premises HSM", "Client-side encryption with keys stored in the cloud provider''s secrets manager"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Per NIST SP 800-57 Part 1 and CSA Cloud Controls Matrix DSI-02, Hold Your Own Key (HYOK) with an on-premises HSM ensures the organization retains sole custody of encryption keys outside the cloud provider''s infrastructure. HYOK is correct because keys never leave the organization''s physical control, enabling immediate revocation of cloud access. While BYOK (choice B) allows customer-generated keys, once uploaded to the provider''s KMS the provider can technically access them, failing to meet the exclusive-control requirement.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Key rotation lifecycle practice
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In April 2026, Whitmore Healthcare encrypts all patient records using AES-256 and manages keys through an on-premises key management system. The security team implements a policy to replace active encryption keys at fixed intervals without re-encrypting all historical data. Which key management practice is being implemented?', '["Key revocation", "Key escrow", "Key derivation", "Key rotation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Per NIST SP 800-57 Part 1 Section 5.3, key rotation is the practice of periodically replacing active cryptographic keys with new ones to limit the exposure window if a key is compromised. Key rotation is correct because it cycles encryption keys at defined intervals while previously encrypted data remains protected under older key versions. Unlike key revocation (choice A), which permanently disables a compromised key and may render data inaccessible, rotation is a proactive lifecycle practice that maintains continuous availability.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: hard / L4 — Homomorphic encryption evaluation
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In March 2026, Crestview Analytics partners with a hospital network to analyze patient outcomes. Regulatory counsel requires that the hospital''s protected health information remain encrypted throughout the entire computation — Crestview must never access plaintext data at any point. The security architect evaluates encryption technologies that allow mathematical operations on ciphertext. Which technology best meets this requirement?', '["Homomorphic encryption", "Transparent data encryption (TDE)", "Format-preserving encryption", "Transport Layer Security (TLS)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Per NIST IR 8214, homomorphic encryption allows computations to be performed directly on encrypted data without decryption, producing encrypted results that match operations on the plaintext. This is correct because it enables Crestview to process hospital data while it remains encrypted end-to-end, satisfying the regulatory requirement. Unlike TDE (choice B), which only protects data at rest on disk and requires decryption during query processing, homomorphic encryption maintains encryption throughout computation.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Zero-knowledge proofs for compliance verification
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In January 2026, Aegis Compliance Solutions must prove to regulators that its client data meets specific threshold criteria without revealing the underlying data values. The privacy architect evaluates a cryptographic protocol that allows one party to prove a statement is true without conveying any information beyond the statement''s validity. Which technology should the architect recommend?', '["Differential privacy", "Zero-knowledge proof", "Data masking", "Secure hashing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Per NIST IR 8214 Rev. 1, zero-knowledge proofs (ZKPs) are cryptographic protocols enabling a prover to demonstrate knowledge of a value or condition to a verifier without disclosing the underlying data. ZKPs are correct because they allow Aegis to prove regulatory compliance thresholds are met without exposing actual data values. Unlike differential privacy (choice A), which adds statistical noise to dataset outputs to protect individual records, ZKPs provide deterministic proof of specific statements without any data disclosure.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: hard / L3 — Database activity monitoring residual risk analysis
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In February 2026, Meridian Bank deploys a database activity monitoring (DAM) solution that captures all SQL queries against its core banking database in real time. The DAM generates alerts for anomalous query patterns but does not block transactions. The security team also relies on network-based DLP at the perimeter. Which residual risk is MOST likely in this configuration?', '["Unencrypted data at rest on the database server", "Failure to detect insider threats accessing data through the application tier", "Authorized database users exfiltrating data through approved query interfaces", "Inability to generate audit logs for compliance reporting"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Per COBIT 2019 DSS05 and NIST SP 800-53 AU-12, database activity monitoring detects anomalous query patterns but cannot prevent authorized users from executing legitimate queries that extract sensitive data within their access rights. This is the most likely residual risk because DAM in monitor-only mode alerts on anomalies but does not block approved query activity. Unlike choice B, DAM does capture application-layer queries and can detect insider access — the gap is in blocking authorized but malicious use, not detection.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Data sovereignty and cross-border transfer impact
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In March 2026, Evergreen Financial Group stores EU customer data in a cloud region located in Frankfurt. The company considers replicating this data to a US-based disaster recovery region to improve recovery time objectives. The privacy officer raises concerns about cross-border data transfer. Which impact is MOST likely if the replication proceeds without additional safeguards?', '["Violation of PCI DSS encryption requirements", "Loss of data availability during failover", "Degradation of encryption key strength", "Non-compliance with GDPR data transfer restrictions"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Per GDPR Articles 44-49, transferring personal data of EU residents to countries outside the European Economic Area requires adequate safeguards such as Standard Contractual Clauses or an adequacy decision. Non-compliance with GDPR transfer restrictions is correct because replicating EU customer data to a US region without mechanisms like SCCs or Binding Corporate Rules violates cross-border transfer rules. Unlike PCI DSS concerns (choice A), which focus on encryption rather than data residency, GDPR specifically governs where personal data may be stored and processed.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Column-level encryption vs row-level security
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In April 2026, Ridgeline Insurance stores policyholder Social Security numbers and claim amounts in the same database table. The security architect must protect SSNs so that even database administrators cannot read them in plaintext, while allowing claims analysts to query claim amounts freely. Which approach BEST addresses both requirements?', '["Column-level encryption on the SSN column with separate key access controls", "Row-level security filtering by user role", "Transparent data encryption on the entire database", "Dynamic data masking on all columns"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Per NIST SP 800-111 and SP 800-53 SC-28, column-level encryption encrypts specific columns so that only users with the decryption key can access plaintext values, while leaving other columns queryable. This is correct because encrypting the SSN column prevents even DBAs without the key from reading plaintext SSNs while claims analysts can still query unencrypted claim amounts. Unlike row-level security (choice B), which filters entire rows by user role but does not encrypt individual column values, column-level encryption provides cryptographic protection at the field level.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Differential privacy vs k-anonymity evaluation
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In March 2026, Atlas Health Research publishes a dataset of 500,000 patient treatment outcomes for academic researchers. The privacy team must ensure individual patients cannot be re-identified even if an attacker possesses auxiliary demographic data, while maintaining high statistical utility for aggregate analyses. Which approach is MOST appropriate?', '["K-anonymity with l-diversity", "Differential privacy with calibrated noise injection", "Static data masking of all identifier fields", "Pseudonymization with a reversible mapping table"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Per NIST SP 800-188, differential privacy provides mathematically provable privacy guarantees by injecting calibrated statistical noise, protecting against re-identification even with auxiliary data. This is correct because differential privacy''s formal epsilon guarantee bounds disclosure risk regardless of attacker knowledge, while preserving aggregate statistical utility. Unlike k-anonymity with l-diversity (choice A), which is vulnerable to composition attacks when an adversary links multiple released datasets, differential privacy maintains its guarantees under arbitrary auxiliary information.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Transparent data encryption (TDE) application
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In January 2026, Summit Credit Union enables a database feature that automatically encrypts data files, log files, and backup files on disk without requiring changes to existing application queries. The encryption and decryption occur transparently at the storage engine level. Which technology has the credit union enabled?', '["Column-level encryption", "Application-layer encryption", "Transparent data encryption (TDE)", "Client-side field-level encryption"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Per NIST SP 800-111, transparent data encryption (TDE) encrypts database files at the storage level, protecting data at rest without requiring application modifications. TDE is correct because it operates at the database engine layer, automatically encrypting and decrypting data during disk I/O operations transparently to applications. Unlike column-level encryption (choice A), which targets specific fields and requires schema changes and key management per column, TDE encrypts the entire database storage layer without altering queries or application logic.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — SSD secure deletion challenges
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In February 2026, Vanguard Tech Solutions decommissions 150 solid-state drives (SSDs) that stored confidential source code. The IT manager plans to use a single-pass overwrite to sanitize the drives before disposal. The security auditor objects, noting that SSDs handle write operations differently than traditional hard drives. What is the MOST likely reason the auditor objects?', '["Single-pass overwrite is prohibited by NIST SP 800-88 for any storage media type", "SSDs cannot be overwritten because they use read-only memory chips", "AES-256 encryption prevents overwrite tools from accessing SSD sectors", "SSD wear-leveling algorithms may leave data remnants in inaccessible spare blocks"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Per NIST SP 800-88 Rev. 1, SSDs use wear-leveling algorithms that distribute writes across flash cells and maintain spare blocks, meaning a standard overwrite pass may not reach all physical locations where data was stored. This is correct because wear-leveling creates inaccessible areas that overwrite utilities cannot address, potentially leaving recoverable data remnants. Unlike choice A, NIST SP 800-88 does not prohibit single-pass overwrites universally — it specifically notes their limitations for flash-based media and recommends cryptographic erase or physical destruction.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — Key escrow single-point-of-failure risk
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In April 2026, Ironbridge Defense Contractors implements a key escrow system where copies of all employee encryption keys are deposited with the corporate security office and a government-designated third party. The CISO must evaluate the security implications of this arrangement for the organization''s threat model. Which risk is the MOST significant concern with key escrow?', '["A compromise of the escrow agent creates a single point of failure exposing all escrowed keys", "Key escrow prevents employees from using asymmetric encryption algorithms", "Escrowed keys cannot be rotated without replacing all previously encrypted data", "The escrow process weakens the mathematical strength of the encryption algorithm"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Per NIST SP 800-57 Part 1 Section 8.4, key escrow concentrates copies of cryptographic keys with a trusted third party, creating a high-value target whose compromise exposes all escrowed keys simultaneously. This is the most significant risk because a single breach of the escrow agent provides an attacker access to every deposited key, undermining the entire encryption infrastructure. Unlike choice D, the escrow process does not alter the mathematical properties of the algorithm itself — it introduces an operational custody risk rather than a cryptographic weakness.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: easy / L2 — Full disk encryption (FDE) application
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In January 2026, Hawthorne Legal Group requires that all company laptops encrypt the entire hard drive contents, including the operating system and temporary files, to protect data if a device is lost or stolen. Which technology meets this requirement?', '["File-level encryption", "Full disk encryption (FDE)", "Network-layer encryption", "Database-level encryption"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Per NIST SP 800-111, full disk encryption (FDE) encrypts the entire contents of a storage device, including the operating system, applications, and temporary files, protecting all data at rest if the device is physically lost. FDE is correct because it provides comprehensive volume-level protection without requiring users to select individual files for encryption. Unlike file-level encryption (choice A), which only protects specifically designated files and may leave temporary files, swap space, and OS data exposed, FDE covers the complete disk.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — End-to-end encryption metadata exposure analysis
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In March 2026, Prism Communications deploys end-to-end encryption (E2EE) for its enterprise messaging platform. Messages are encrypted on the sender''s device and decrypted only on the recipient''s device. The security team notes that metadata — including sender identity, recipient identity, timestamps, and message frequency — remains visible to the platform operator. What is the MOST significant security implication of this metadata exposure?', '["Attackers can decrypt message contents using metadata correlation", "The encryption algorithm is weakened by metadata transmission", "Traffic analysis can reveal communication patterns and relationships without accessing message content", "E2EE is ineffective because metadata exposes the encryption keys"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Per NIST SP 800-53 SC-8 and the NIST Privacy Framework, end-to-end encryption protects message content but does not conceal metadata such as communication patterns, timing, and participant identities. Traffic analysis is the most significant implication because an adversary can map organizational relationships, identify communication frequency, and infer sensitive activities without decrypting any messages. Unlike choice A, metadata correlation does not enable decryption of ciphertext — it reveals behavioral patterns that are independently valuable for intelligence gathering.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Digital watermarking for leak attribution
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In February 2026, Centurion Asset Management embeds unique invisible identifiers into each copy of a confidential investment strategy document distributed to its 12 portfolio managers. When a leaked copy surfaces on a competitor''s server, the security team traces the embedded identifier back to a specific recipient. Which data protection technique was used?', '["Data loss prevention content inspection", "Steganographic message hiding", "Format-preserving encryption", "Digital watermarking"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Per NIST SP 800-53 AU-10 and SI-4, digital watermarking embeds unique, typically invisible identifiers into documents or media that persist through copying and distribution, enabling traceability to specific recipients. Watermarking is correct because each copy received a unique identifier allowing forensic attribution when the leaked document was recovered. Unlike steganography (choice B), which hides secret messages within carrier files for covert communication, digital watermarking is specifically designed for provenance tracking and leak attribution rather than hidden data transmission.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — SFTP vs FTPS protocol selection
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In April 2026, Crestmore Accounting Firm must securely transfer large audit workpaper files to a client''s server. The firm''s IT policy requires that the file transfer protocol operate over a single encrypted channel using SSH for authentication and encryption. Which protocol meets this requirement?', '["SFTP (SSH File Transfer Protocol)", "FTPS (FTP over TLS)", "Standard FTP with password authentication", "HTTP with basic authentication"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Per NIST SP 800-53 SC-8 and SC-13, SFTP operates as a subsystem of the SSH protocol, providing encrypted file transfer over a single connection with SSH-based authentication and encryption. SFTP is correct because it uses the SSH transport layer for both authentication and data encryption through one connection channel. Unlike FTPS (choice B), which layers TLS encryption over the traditional FTP protocol and requires separate control and data channels with additional firewall configuration, SFTP operates through a single port using the SSH framework.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: easy / L2 — Password salting mechanism
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In March 2026, Beacon Software Inc. adds a unique random value to each user''s password before applying the hash function and storing the result. This prevents two users with identical passwords from having identical stored hash values. What is this random value called?', '["An initialization vector", "A cryptographic salt", "A single-use nonce", "A server-side pepper"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Per NIST SP 800-63B Section 5.1.1.2, a salt is a unique random value appended to each password before hashing to ensure that identical passwords produce different hash outputs. A salt is correct because it defeats precomputed dictionary attacks (rainbow tables) by making each stored hash unique even for duplicate passwords. Unlike an initialization vector (choice A), which is used with symmetric encryption algorithms to randomize ciphertext blocks, a salt is specifically associated with password hashing operations.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Symmetric encryption definition
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'Which type of encryption uses the same key for both encrypting and decrypting data?', '["Asymmetric encryption", "Public key encryption", "Symmetric encryption", "Digital signature encryption"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Per NIST SP 800-175B, symmetric encryption uses a single shared secret key for both encryption and decryption operations. Symmetric encryption is correct because the same cryptographic key performs both the forward (encrypt) and reverse (decrypt) transformations. Unlike asymmetric encryption (choice A), which uses a mathematically related key pair — a public key for encryption and a private key for decryption — symmetric encryption relies on one shared key that both parties must securely possess.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Trusted Platform Module (TPM) purpose
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'What is the PRIMARY function of a Trusted Platform Module (TPM) chip installed on a computer motherboard?', '["Accelerating network data throughput", "Filtering malicious email attachments", "Compressing encrypted files for storage efficiency", "Providing hardware-based secure storage for cryptographic keys"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Per NIST SP 800-147 and the Trusted Computing Group specification, a Trusted Platform Module (TPM) is a dedicated hardware chip that securely generates, stores, and manages cryptographic keys and platform integrity measurements. TPM is correct because its primary function is providing a tamper-resistant hardware root of trust for key storage and platform attestation. Unlike network throughput acceleration (choice A), which is handled by network interface hardware, TPMs are specifically designed for cryptographic security functions.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) purpose
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'What is the PRIMARY purpose of a Public Key Infrastructure (PKI)?', '["Managing digital certificates and trust relationships between certificate authorities and end entities", "Encrypting all network traffic using symmetric key algorithms", "Providing physical security controls for data center access", "Monitoring database queries for unauthorized access patterns"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Per NIST SP 800-32, Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) is a framework for managing digital certificates, public-private key pairs, and trust hierarchies between certificate authorities and relying parties. PKI is correct because it establishes the infrastructure for issuing, distributing, validating, and revoking digital certificates that enable authenticated communications. Unlike symmetric key encryption (choice B), which involves shared secret keys, PKI specifically manages asymmetric cryptographic identities and the certificate lifecycle.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Three states of data
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'What are the three recognized states of data that information security controls must protect?', '["Data in creation, data in review, and data in archive", "Data at rest, data in transit, and data in use", "Data in backup, data in production, and data in testing", "Data in cloud, data on premises, and data in hybrid"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Per NIST SP 800-53 and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework, the three states of data are data at rest (stored), data in transit (moving across networks), and data in use (being processed in memory). This is correct because security controls must address all three states to achieve comprehensive data protection. Unlike choice A, which describes document lifecycle phases rather than technical data states, the three data states represent the conditions under which data exists and requires distinct protection mechanisms.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Digital certificate binding
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'What does a digital certificate issued by a certificate authority (CA) primarily bind together?', '["An IP address and a MAC address", "A username and a password hash", "A public key and the identity of its owner", "An encryption algorithm and a key length"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Per NIST SP 800-32 and the X.509 standard, a digital certificate binds a public key to the identity of its owner, with the binding authenticated by the digital signature of a trusted certificate authority. This is correct because the certificate assures relying parties that a specific public key belongs to the named entity. Unlike a username-password association (choice B), which is an authentication credential pair, a digital certificate provides cryptographically verified identity-to-key binding through a trusted third party.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: medium / L3 — IRM analog capture limitation analysis
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In April 2026, Ashford Consulting applies Information Rights Management (IRM) to confidential strategy documents, restricting recipients to view-only access with no printing, forwarding, or copying. An analyst discovers that a recipient took photographs of the document displayed on their monitor using a personal smartphone. What does this incident MOST likely demonstrate about IRM controls?', '["IRM encryption was improperly configured on the document", "The IRM licensing server failed to enforce the access policy", "The recipient''s device lacked a compatible IRM client application", "IRM cannot prevent analog capture methods that bypass digital enforcement"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Per NIST SP 800-53 AC-21 and COBIT 2019 DSS05.02, Information Rights Management enforces digital usage restrictions on documents but cannot control physical or analog capture methods such as screen photography. This is correct because IRM operates within the digital environment and has no mechanism to prevent a user from photographing a displayed screen with an external device. Unlike IRM misconfiguration (choice A), the control functioned as designed — the limitation is inherent in all digital rights enforcement, which cannot extend to analog capture vectors.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Certificate pinning for mobile apps
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In March 2026, Keystone Mobile Banking deploys a mobile application that stores a predefined set of server certificate hashes within the app code. When connecting to the bank''s API, the app compares the server''s presented certificate against these stored hashes and rejects any non-matching connection. Which technique is Keystone implementing?', '["Certificate pinning", "Mutual TLS authentication", "Certificate transparency logging", "Online certificate status checking"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Per NIST SP 800-52 Rev. 2, certificate pinning associates a host with its expected public key or certificate hash, allowing applications to reject connections presenting unexpected certificates even if signed by a trusted CA. Pinning is correct because the app validates the server''s certificate against hardcoded hashes, preventing man-in-the-middle attacks using rogue certificates. Unlike mutual TLS (choice B), which requires both client and server to present certificates for bidirectional authentication, certificate pinning is a client-side validation of the server''s specific certificate identity.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Automated data discovery and classification
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In February 2026, Oakmont Enterprises deploys a tool that scans file servers and cloud storage repositories, automatically identifying and tagging documents containing sensitive information such as Social Security numbers and financial account data based on predefined content patterns. Which data protection capability is Oakmont implementing?', '["Data loss prevention policy enforcement", "Backup and disaster recovery scanning", "Database activity monitoring and alerting", "Automated data discovery and classification"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Per NIST SP 800-53 RA-2 and COBIT 2019 APO01.06, automated data discovery and classification tools scan repositories to identify, categorize, and label sensitive data based on content patterns, regular expressions, and machine learning models. This is correct because the tool identifies and tags sensitive data across storage locations using predefined patterns. Unlike DLP policy enforcement (choice A), which actively blocks or restricts data movement based on existing classifications, data discovery and classification is the prerequisite step of finding and labeling sensitive data before enforcement rules can be applied.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — Network segmentation effect on lateral movement
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In April 2026, Garrison Healthcare segments its network so that electronic health record (EHR) servers reside in a dedicated VLAN accessible only through a firewall with strict access control lists. General employee workstations are on a separate VLAN with no direct route to EHR servers. A phishing attack compromises three employee workstations on the general VLAN. What is the MOST likely effect of the network segmentation on this incident?', '["The compromised workstations automatically receive emergency security patches", "The phishing malware is quarantined by the VLAN configuration itself", "The attacker''s lateral movement to EHR servers is restricted by the inter-VLAN firewall", "The EHR data is automatically encrypted by the network segmentation controls"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Per NIST SP 800-53 SC-7 and NIST SP 800-125B, network segmentation isolates sensitive assets into separate network zones, restricting lateral movement by requiring traffic to pass through enforcement points such as firewalls. This is correct because the inter-VLAN firewall prevents compromised workstations from directly reaching EHR servers, containing the blast radius of the phishing attack. Unlike choice B, VLANs provide logical separation but do not detect or quarantine malware — they restrict network paths, forcing traffic through firewall inspection points.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Steganography definition
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'What is steganography in the context of information security?', '["A method of encrypting data using public and private key pairs", "A technique for compressing files to reduce storage requirements", "A protocol for secure data transmission over the internet", "The practice of hiding secret data within ordinary non-secret files or messages"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Per NIST SP 800-53 SI-4, steganography is the practice of concealing secret information within ordinary carrier files such as images, audio, or text so that the existence of the hidden data is not apparent. This is correct because steganography hides the very existence of the communication, unlike encryption which makes content unreadable but visibly present. Unlike public key encryption (choice A), which transforms plaintext into visible ciphertext, steganography embeds data imperceptibly within innocuous-looking carrier media.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — NIST SP 800-57 key state transition
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In January 2026, Redstone Financial Group follows NIST SP 800-57 to manage its encryption key lifecycle. A symmetric key used for encrypting transaction records has reached its designated cryptoperiod expiration date. The security team transitions the key so it can only decrypt existing records but not encrypt new data. Which key state has this key entered?', '["Active", "Deactivated", "Suspended", "Destroyed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Per NIST SP 800-57 Part 1 Section 7.2, a deactivated key has passed its cryptoperiod and is no longer used for applying cryptographic protection (encryption) but may still be used for processing previously protected data (decryption). Deactivated is correct because the key retains decrypt-only capability after its active encryption period expires. Unlike a suspended key (choice C), which is temporarily taken out of normal use due to a suspected compromise and may be reactivated, a deactivated key has permanently transitioned past its encryption cryptoperiod.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Secure multiparty computation for collaborative analysis
(5, 'Data Protection Technologies', 'In March 2026, three competing banks — Clearwater National, Pacific Mutual, and Summit Federal — want to jointly compute aggregate fraud statistics across their combined transaction data without any bank revealing its individual transaction records to the others. Which technology BEST enables this collaborative analysis while preserving data confidentiality?', '["Secure multiparty computation (SMPC)", "Centralized data warehouse with role-based access controls", "Federated database with shared read permissions", "Encrypted email exchange of summary reports"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Per NIST IR 8214, secure multiparty computation (SMPC) enables multiple parties to jointly compute a function over their combined inputs while keeping each party''s input private from the others. SMPC is correct because it allows the three banks to calculate aggregate fraud statistics without any single bank accessing another''s raw transaction data. Unlike a centralized data warehouse (choice B), which requires all parties to share their data with a central repository and trust its access controls, SMPC maintains mathematical guarantees that individual inputs remain confidential throughout computation.', 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
